 ],
 "literal": "Of the great sea shore, the small white gull, / in the deep water of the dark backwater, having searched for prey and eaten, / in the flower-fragrant grove roosts, with the lord of such a shore, / we bound, bound is our friendship! / To untie, it is difficult; that is knotted and settled indeed!",
 "translation": "Our friendship with the lord of the shore—where the small white gull of the great sea-coast seeks and eats its prey in the deep waters of the dark backwater, and then roosts in the flower-fragrant grove—was bound by us, and bound it remains. It is impossible to untie; it is firmly knotted and settled forever.",
 "modern": "பெரிய கடல் கரையைச் சேர்ந்த சிறிய வெண்மையான கடற்காகம், ஆழமான நீரையுடைய கரிய கழியில் தன் இரையைத் தேடித் தின்றுவிட்டு, மலர்கள் மணக்கும் சோலையில் தங்கும் துறைமுகத் தலைவனுடன் நாம் கொண்ட நட்பு, நம்மால் பிணைக்கப்பட்டது; அது உறுதியாகப் பிணைக்கப்பட்டுள்ளது. அதனை அவிழ்ப்பது அரிது; அது முடிந்து நிலைபெற்றுவிட்டது.",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the heroine to her confidante, reassuring her of the unbreakable strength of her love for the hero. The setting belongs to the neytal (coastal) landscape, where a small white sea-gull feeds in the deep, dark backwaters and then finds rest in a fragrant grove. This image of the bird finding refuge mirrors the hero, who finds peace and fulfillment in the heroine's love. The heroine emphasizes that their bond (naṭpu) was mutually bound (yāttēm) and is now a permanent knot. It is described as 'aviḻttaṟku aritu' (impossible to untie), showing her absolute certainty in their relationship despite any temporary separation.",
